\title{The Splitting-Type Channel of a Cyclic Cubic Field:\\
Full Pinning at Conductor 13, Exact Pairing Defects,\\
and a Structural Which-Factor Wall}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{22 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study the \emph{splitting-type channel} of a cyclic number field: the map sending a rational
prime $p$ to its decomposition type in a fixed abelian extension of $\Fq$, viewed as a channel
whose input is the residue class of $p$ modulo the conductor. For the cyclic cubic field of
conductor $13$ --- and, as we show, for every cyclic cubic field of prime conductor $f$ with
$3 \mid f-1$ --- the channel has exactly two output symbols, \emph{split} and \emph{inert}, with
probabilities $1/3$ and $2/3$, Shannon entropy $H(T)=\log_2 3-\tfrac23=0.9182958\ldots$ bits, and
is \emph{fully pinned}: $I(p \bmod 13; T)=H(T)$ exactly, with zero conditional entropy. For
semiprimes $n=pq$ we prove $I\bigl(T(n);\{T(p),T(q)\}\bigr)=\log_2 3-\tfrac{10}{9}$, so that the
pairing defect is the rational number $4/9$, and we prove a rigidity theorem: at prime degree $q$
the defect is rational if and only if $q\in\{2,3\}$, and the type entropy is rational if and only
if $q=2$; in particular $\log_2 3-2/3$ is irrational. We further prove the \emph{which-factor
wall}: the mutual information between all observable data attached to $n$ and the identity of
which factor carries which type is exactly zero, with optimal decoding success exactly $1/2$; this
follows from a general principle about groups acting freely and transitively on a hidden label
while fixing every observable. Finally, invariance under uniform covers shows that every
functional of the type distribution --- in particular every R\'enyi order,
$H_a=(1-a)^{-1}\log_2\bigl((1/3)^a+(2/3)^a\bigr)$, with $H_0=1$ and $H_2=\log_2(9/5)$, the ordering
$H_2<H_1$ reducing to $108<125$ --- is conductor-independent, and we describe the subfield tower at
conductor $13$ as an information filtration with multiplicativity $H(T_{mn})=H(T_m)+H(T_n)$ for
coprime $m,n$ and prime-power saturation $H(T_{p^e})=C(p)(1-p^{-e})$, $C(p)=p\log_2 p/(p-1)-\log_2(p-1)$.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} cyclic cubic field, conductor, splitting type, equidistribution,
Shannon entropy, mutual information, R\'enyi entropy, semiprime, irrationality.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The question}
Class field theory determines, for an abelian extension $K/\Fq$ of conductor $f$, how a rational
prime $p \nmid f$ decomposes in $K$: the answer depends only on $p \bmod f$, through the Artin map
$(\Z/f)^\times \twoheadrightarrow \Gal(K/\Fq)$. Combined with Dirichlet's theorem on the
equidistribution of primes in arithmetic progressions, this turns the decomposition type of a
``random prime'' into a genuine random variable with computable law. We ask information-theoretic
questions about that random variable: how many bits does the type carry, how many are recoverable
from the residue class, what survives multiplication of two primes, and what is annihilated by it.

For the cyclic cubic case the answers are unusually clean: every quantity is either an exact
rational number, an explicit rational combination of logarithms which we prove irrational, or an
exact zero which we prove is enforced by symmetry rather than by numerical accident.

\subsection{The object of study}
Fix a prime $f$ with $3 \mid f-1$ (the smallest examples being $7, 13, 19, 31, 37$) and let $K_f$
be the unique cyclic cubic subfield of $\Fq(\zeta_f)$. For $f=13$, $K_{13}$ is generated by a root
of $x^3+x^2-4x+1=0$, the field of cubic Gaussian periods of the thirteenth roots of unity, of
discriminant $169$ and conductor $13$. A prime $p \neq f$ either splits completely into three
primes of residue degree $1$, or is inert with residue degree $3$; the dichotomy is decided by
whether $p$ is a cube modulo $f$. For $f=13$ the cubes are $\{1,5,8,12\}$, four of twelve classes.
We write $T$ for the resulting \emph{type} random variable and $H$ for Shannon entropy in bits.

\subsection{Two numerical corrections}
Preliminary sampling on this channel circulated the values $H(T)\approx 0.9192$,
$I_{\mathrm{pair}}\approx 0.4702$ and a which-factor information of about $10^{-4}$. The exact
values are $H(T)=\log_2 3-2/3=0.9182958\ldots<0.9187$ and
$I_{\mathrm{pair}}=\log_2 3-10/9=0.4738514\ldots>0.4737$, and the which-factor information is
exactly $0$. The first two discrepancies are of the size expected from finite-sample plug-in
entropy estimation; the third is the classical upward bias of plug-in mutual-information
estimators, which report a small positive number where the truth is zero.

\section{Definitions}

\begin{definition}[Entropy and mutual information]
For a random variable $X$ on a finite set with law $(p_v)$, $H(X)=-\sum_v p_v\log_2 p_v$,
$H(X\mid Y)=\sum_y \Pr[Y=y]H(X\mid Y=y)$ and $I(X;Y)=H(X)-H(X\mid Y)\ge 0$. We write
$h(x)=-x\log_2 x-(1-x)\log_2(1-x)$ for the binary entropy function, with $h(0)=h(1)=0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[R\'enyi entropy]
For $a\ge 0$, $a\ne 1$, $H_a(X)=\frac{1}{1-a}\log_2\sum_v p_v^{\,a}$; $H_1$ is defined by
continuity and equals $H$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Residue group and type map]
Let $f$ be prime and $d \mid f-1$. The Artin map identifies $\Gal(K_{f,d}/\Fq)$ with
$\Gamma_d=(\Z/f)^\times/\bigl((\Z/f)^\times\bigr)^d\cong C_d$, where $K_{f,d}$ is the unique
degree-$d$ subfield of $\Fq(\zeta_f)$. For $p\ne f$ let $g(p)\in\Gamma_d$ be the image of
$p \bmod f$. The \emph{type} is $T_d(p)=\ord\bigl(g(p)\bigr)$, a divisor of $d$: it is the residue
degree of any prime above $p$, and $p$ splits into $d/T_d(p)$ primes. For $d=q$ prime the type has
two values, $1$ (\emph{split}) and $q$ (\emph{inert}).
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Splitting-type channel]
Fix $d$. The \emph{splitting-type channel} has input $R=p\bmod f$, uniform on $(\Z/f)^\times$, and
output $T=T_d(p)$, a deterministic function of $R$. By Dirichlet's theorem this is the correct
asymptotic model for a prime drawn uniformly from $\{p\le X: p \ne f\}$ as $X\to\infty$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Uniform cover]
A surjection $\pi:A\to B$ of finite sets with uniform measures is a \emph{uniform cover} if all
fibres $\pi^{-1}(b)$ have the same cardinality; equivalently, $\pi$ pushes the uniform measure on
$A$ forward to the uniform measure on $B$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Semiprime model]
For $n=pq$ with $g(p),g(q)$ independent and uniform in $\Gamma_q$, the \emph{observable} is the
type $T(n)$ of the product, determined by $g(n)=g(p)+g(q)$; the \emph{hidden pair} is the unordered
multiset $\{T(p),T(q)\}$; the \emph{hidden ordering} records which factor realises which type.
\end{definition}

\section{Full pinning at conductor 13}

\begin{theorem}[Full pinning]\label{thm:pin}
Let $K$ be the cyclic cubic field of conductor $13$ and $p \ne 13$ a prime. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $p$ splits completely in $K$ iff $p\bmod 13\in\{1,5,8,12\}$, and is inert otherwise; there
are exactly two types;
\item $\Pr[T=\text{split}]=1/3$ and $\Pr[T=\text{inert}]=2/3$;
\item $H(T)=\log_2 3-\tfrac23=0.9182958\ldots$ bits;
\item $H(T\mid p\bmod 13)=0$, hence $I(p\bmod 13;T)=H(T)$ exactly.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
(1) $(\Z/13)^\times$ is cyclic of order $12$, generated by $2$; the cubes form the unique subgroup
of order $4$, namely $\{1,5,8,12\}=\{2^{3k}\}$. The Artin map sends $p$ to its class in the
quotient $C_3$, and $p$ splits iff that class is trivial. Only $p=13$ ramifies. (2) Four of twelve
classes are cubes, and residues are equidistributed. (3)
$-\frac13\log_2\frac13-\frac23\log_2\frac23=\log_2 3-\frac23$. (4) $T$ is a function of the
residue, so every conditional law is a point mass.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Part (4) is \emph{full pinning}: the channel is lossless, with no approximation and no error term.
\end{remark}

\begin{proposition}[Arithmetic grounding]
The cosets of the cubes in $(\Z/13)^\times$ are $\{1,5,8,12\}$, $\{2,3,10,11\}$ and
$\{4,6,7,9\}$, and residues multiply by adding cosets in $C_3$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Conductor independence]\label{thm:condind}
Let $f,f'$ be primes with $3\mid f-1$ and $3\mid f'-1$. The cubic splitting-type channels at $f$
and $f'$ have identical output laws $(1/3,2/3)$, identical entropy $\log_2 3-2/3$, and both are
fully pinned.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The quotient $(\Z/f)^\times\to\Gamma_3\cong C_3$ is a group homomorphism onto a group of order $3$,
so its fibres are cosets of the kernel, all of size $(f-1)/3$: a uniform cover. The push-forward of
the uniform input law is therefore the uniform law on $C_3$, independent of $f$; the type is
\emph{split} on the identity and \emph{inert} elsewhere.
\end{proof}

\section{The semiprime pairing channel}

Write $C_3=\{0,1,2\}$ additively, with \emph{split} $\leftrightarrow 0$, and $g(pq)=g(p)+g(q)$.

\begin{lemma}[Nine-configuration table]\label{lem:nine}
With $g(p),g(q)$ independent and uniform on $C_3$:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
hidden pair & probability & conditional law of $T(n)$ & conditional entropy\\
\midrule
$\{\text{split},\text{split}\}$ & $1/9$ & split w.p.\ $1$ & $0$\\
$\{\text{split},\text{inert}\}$ & $4/9$ & inert w.p.\ $1$ & $0$\\
$\{\text{inert},\text{inert}\}$ & $4/9$ & split w.p.\ $1/2$ & $1$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
Moreover the marginal law of $T(n)$ is again $(1/3,2/3)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$(0,0)$ has mass $1/9$ and sum $0$; the four configurations with exactly one zero coordinate have
total mass $4/9$ and nonzero sum; among the four with both coordinates nonzero, the sum vanishes
exactly at $(1,2)$ and $(2,1)$. The marginal follows since $g(n)$ is uniform on $C_3$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact pairing information and rational defect]\label{thm:pair}
For the cubic channel,
\[
I\bigl(T(n);\{T(p),T(q)\}\bigr)=\log_2 3-\tfrac{10}{9}=0.4738514\ldots,
\qquad H(T)-I_{\mathrm{pair}}=\tfrac49 \text{ exactly.}
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:nine}, $H\bigl(T(n)\mid\{T(p),T(q)\}\bigr)=\tfrac19\cdot 0+\tfrac49\cdot 0+\tfrac49\cdot 1=\tfrac49$,
while $H(T(n))=\log_2 3-\tfrac23$; subtract.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Prime-degree defect formula]\label{thm:defect}
For a prime degree $q$, with $\Pr[\text{split}]=1/q$,
\[
H(T_q)=\log_2 q-\frac{q-1}{q}\log_2(q-1),\qquad
H(T_q)-I_{\mathrm{pair}}(q)=\frac{(q-1)^2}{q^2}\,h\!\left(\frac{1}{q-1}\right).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The hidden pair is $\{\text{split},\text{split}\}$ with probability $1/q^2$ (observable
determined), mixed with probability $2(q-1)/q^2$ (observable determined), and
$\{\text{inert},\text{inert}\}$ with probability $(q-1)^2/q^2$. In the last case, for each nonzero
$g(p)$ exactly one of the $q-1$ nonzero values of $g(q)$ makes the sum vanish, so the observable is
Bernoulli$(1/(q-1))$. The defect is this conditional entropy. For $q=3$ it is
$\tfrac49 h(\tfrac12)=\tfrac49$; for $q=2$ it is $\tfrac14 h(1)=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
As $q\to\infty$ both $H(T_q)$ and the defect are $\frac{\log_2 q}{q}(1+o(1))$, so
$I_{\mathrm{pair}}(q)=o\bigl(H(T_q)\bigr)$: at large degree, pairing destroys asymptotically all of
the information.
\end{corollary}

\section{Rigidity: which defects are rational?}

\begin{lemma}[Integer-identity clearing]\label{lem:clear}
Let $u>v\ge 1$ be coprime integers and $s,t\ge 1$. If $s\log_2 u-t\log_2 v\in\mathbb{Q}$, then there
are $A\in\Z$ and $b\ge 1$ with $u^{sb}=2^{A}v^{tb}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write the value as $A/b$; then $u^{s}/v^{t}=2^{A/b}$, so $u^{sb}/v^{tb}=2^{A}$, and clearing
denominators gives the identity.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Coprime power obstruction]\label{lem:obstruct}
Let $u>v\ge 1$ be coprime and $s,t,b\ge 1$. If $u$ has an odd prime factor, or if $v>1$ is odd,
then $u^{sb}=2^{A}v^{tb}$ is impossible for every $A\in\Z$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
An odd prime $\ell\mid u$ divides the left-hand side but divides neither $2^{A}$ nor $v$ (by
coprimality), hence not the right-hand side; symmetrically for an odd prime dividing $v$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Rational-defect rigidity]\label{thm:rigid}
Let $q$ be prime.
\begin{enumerate}
\item $H(T_q)=\log_2 q-\frac{q-1}{q}\log_2(q-1)$ is rational iff $q=2$ (value $1$). In particular
$\log_2 3-2/3$ is irrational.
\item The pairing defect $H(T_q)-I_{\mathrm{pair}}(q)$ is rational iff $q\in\{2,3\}$, with values
$0$ and $4/9$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
(1) If $q$ is odd and $H(T_q)$ were rational, then so is $q\log_2 q-(q-1)\log_2(q-1)$;
Lemma~\ref{lem:clear} with the coprime consecutive pair $(q,q-1)$ gives
$q^{qb}=2^{A}(q-1)^{(q-1)b}$, contradicting Lemma~\ref{lem:obstruct} since $q$ is an odd prime. For
$q=2$, $H(T_2)=1$.

(2) By Theorem~\ref{thm:defect} the defect is a nonzero rational multiple of
$B_q=\log_2(q-1)-\frac{q-2}{q-1}\log_2(q-2)$. Here $B_2=0$ and $B_3=1$. For $q\ge 5$, rationality
of $B_q$ forces $(q-1)^{(q-1)b}=2^{A}(q-2)^{(q-2)b}$ with the coprime consecutive pair
$(q-1,q-2)$. If $q-1$ has an odd prime factor, Lemma~\ref{lem:obstruct} applies; otherwise
$q-1=2^{k}$ and $q-2=2^{k}-1\ge 3$ is odd and greater than $1$, so Lemma~\ref{lem:obstruct} applies
in its second form.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
No transcendence theory is needed: unique factorisation and the coprimality of consecutive integers
suffice. The escaping degrees $q\in\{2,3\}$ are exactly those for which the surviving logarithm has
argument $1$ or $2$ and therefore degenerates.
\end{remark}

\begin{corollary}
Both $\log_2 3-2/3$ and $\log_2 3-10/9$ are irrational, yet their difference is exactly $4/9$: the
defect is an exact rational invariant separating two irrational quantities.
\end{corollary}

\section{The which-factor wall}

\begin{definition}
Let $n=pq$, work with the ordered pair $(g(p),g(q))$, and let $W$ record which factor carries which
type. The \emph{observable} $\mathcal{O}$ consists of the product class $g(n)$, the type $T(n)$,
the unordered pair $\{T(p),T(q)\}$ --- indeed of any function of $n$ alone.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Which-factor wall]\label{thm:wall}
$I(\mathcal{O};W)=0$ exactly.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The transposition $(g(p),g(q))\mapsto(g(q),g(p))$ preserves the (exchangeable) law, fixes every
observable --- the product class is symmetric, hence so is $n$ and so is the unordered pair of
types --- and exchanges the two values of $W$. Conditionally on any observation, the law of $W$ is
therefore invariant under swapping its two values, hence uniform; so $H(W\mid\mathcal{O})=H(W)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Decoder half-law]
Every decoder $\hat W=D(\mathcal{O})$, deterministic or randomised, satisfies
$\Pr[\hat W=W]=1/2$ exactly: no strategy does better, and none does worse.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[General cyclic wall]\label{thm:cyclicwall}
Let a finite group $G$ act freely and transitively on a hidden label set $L$, and let the joint law
of (hidden state, observable) be $G$-invariant with the observable $G$-fixed. Then the mutual
information between observable and label is exactly $0$, and optimal decoding succeeds with
probability exactly $1/|L|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For each observation, the conditional law of the label is a $G$-invariant measure on a set with a
transitive $G$-action, hence uniform; freeness gives $|L|=|G|$. So the conditional entropy equals
$\log_2|L|$, the unconditional value.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The $4/9$-bit pairing defect is \emph{quantitative} loss: information exists and is partly
transmitted. The which-factor zero is \emph{structural}: the observable factors through the
quotient by a group acting transitively on the unknown. The two respond differently to
intervention --- the defect shrinks given further observables correlated with the ambiguous
configuration, whereas the wall is impervious to any observable respecting the symmetry.
\end{remark}

\section{Invariance and the whole R\'enyi family}

\begin{proposition}[Push-forward under uniform covers]\label{prop:push}
Let $\pi:A\to B$ be a uniform cover of finite uniform spaces and $\tau:B\to V$ any map. Then
$\tau\circ\pi$ and $\tau$ induce the same law on $V$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
With common fibre size $c$, $|\pi^{-1}(\tau^{-1}(v))|/|A|=c|\tau^{-1}(v)|/(c|B|)=|\tau^{-1}(v)|/|B|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Functional invariance]\label{thm:funcinv}
Under the hypotheses of Proposition~\ref{prop:push}, for every $F:[0,1]\to\mathbb{R}$,
\[
\sum_{v\in V}F\bigl(\Pr[\tau\circ\pi=v]\bigr)=\sum_{v\in V}F\bigl(\Pr[\tau=v]\bigr).
\]
Hence Shannon entropy, all R\'enyi entropies, all min- and max-entropies and all moments of the
type distribution are invariant.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[R\'enyi spectrum of the cubic channel]\label{thm:renyi}
For every admissible conductor and every order $a\ne 1$,
\[
H_a(T)=\frac{1}{1-a}\log_2\left(\left(\tfrac13\right)^{a}+\left(\tfrac23\right)^{a}\right).
\]
In particular $H_0=1$, $H_1=\log_2 3-2/3$, $H_2=\log_2\tfrac95=0.8479969\ldots$ and
$H_\infty=\log_2\tfrac32$. Moreover $H_2<H_1$, an inequality equivalent to $108<125$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The formula is the definition applied to $(1/3,2/3)$, conductor-independent by
Theorem~\ref{thm:funcinv}. For the ordering, $H_2<H_1$ reads $\log_2 3+\tfrac23<\log_2 5$;
multiplying by $3$ gives $\log_2 108<\log_2 125$.
\end{proof}

\section{The subfield tower as an information filtration}

Let $f=13$. The subfields of $\Fq(\zeta_{13})$ have degrees $d\mid 12$, and
$\Pr[T_d=e]=\varphi(e)/d$ for $e\mid d$, since a cyclic group of order $d$ has $\varphi(e)$
elements of order $e$. Hence $H(T_d)=-\sum_{e\mid d}\frac{\varphi(e)}{d}\log_2\frac{\varphi(e)}{d}$.

\begin{proposition}[Filtration]
If $d\mid d'$ then observing in degree $d'$ refines observing in degree $d$, and
$H(T_d)\le H(T_{d'})$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[CRT additivity]\label{thm:crt}
For coprime $m,n$, $H(T_{mn})=H(T_m)+H(T_n)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$C_{mn}\cong C_m\times C_n$ with the uniform measure factorising, and the order of an element is
the product of the orders of its coordinates; entropy is additive on independent pairs.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Prime-power saturation]\label{thm:sat}
For a prime $p$ and $e\ge 1$,
\[
H(T_{p^e})=C(p)\bigl(1-p^{-e}\bigr),\qquad C(p)=\frac{p\log_2 p}{p-1}-\log_2(p-1).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The law is $\Pr[T=p^{0}]=p^{-e}$ and $\Pr[T=p^{i}]=(1-p^{-1})p^{i-e}$ for $1\le i\le e$;
substituting into the entropy sum and evaluating the geometric and arithmetico-geometric sums gives
the closed form, with $C(p)$ the limit as $e\to\infty$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The conductor-13 tower]
With $\eta=\log_2 3-2/3$:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}
\toprule
degree $d$ & $1$ & $2$ & $3$ & $4$ & $6$ & $12$\\
\midrule
$H(T_d)$ & $0$ & $1$ & $\eta\approx0.918296$ & $3/2$ & $1+\eta\approx1.918296$ & $3/2+\eta\approx2.418296$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
The gaps $H(T_2)-H(T_1)=1$, $H(T_4)-H(T_2)=1/2$ and $H(T_{12})-H(T_3)=3/2$ are rational, while every
gap in the $3$-direction equals the irrational $\eta$. Here $C(2)=2$ and
$C(3)=\tfrac32\log_2 3-1=1.3774438\ldots$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Splitting-type oracle.}
Given a prime $p$ and admissible prime conductor $f$: set $r=p\bmod f$ (return \emph{ramified} if
$r=0$), compute $c=r^{(f-1)/3}\bmod f$, and return \emph{split} if $c=1$ and \emph{inert}
otherwise. Correctness is Euler's criterion for cubic residuacity in the cyclic group
$(\Z/f)^\times$. Cost: one reduction plus one modular exponentiation, $O(\log f)$ multiplications;
for $f=13$ a table lookup against $\{1,5,8,12\}$.

\paragraph{Exact channel statistics.}
Given $f$ and $d\mid f-1$: bin the residues by $\ord(g(x))$, obtaining bin sizes
$\varphi(e)(f-1)/d$, and return the law $(\varphi(e)/d)_{e\mid d}$ with its entropies. Naively
$O(f\log f)$; but by Theorem~\ref{thm:condind} the closed form $\varphi(e)/d$ makes the cost $O(d)$
with no reference to $f$ at all.

\paragraph{Semiprime pairing analysis.}
For prime degree $q$: build the $q\times q$ joint table of $(g(p),g(q))$ with cells of mass
$q^{-2}$, fold to the hidden pair and the observable, and return $H(\text{obs})$,
$H(\text{obs}\mid\text{pair})$ and their difference. Cost $O(q^2)$, or $O(1)$ via
Theorem~\ref{thm:defect}; the tabular version is best used as a check of the closed form.

\section{Applications and discussion}

\paragraph{Limits on congruence-based factoring heuristics.}
Any procedure whose entire input is a set of local symbols of $n$ --- splitting types in abelian
extensions, residue classes, Legendre- or Jacobi-style symbols --- is a function on the quotient by
factor permutation, hence subject to Theorem~\ref{thm:cyclicwall}: it yields exactly zero
information about which factor is which. Progress must break the invariance rather than sharpen the
estimator.

\paragraph{Exact benchmarks for entropy estimation.}
The channel supplies test problems with closed-form answers of three flavours: an irrational
entropy $\log_2 3-2/3$, an irrational mutual information $\log_2 3-10/9$ and a structural zero. An
estimator returning $0.9192$ for the first and $10^{-4}$ for the third exhibits precisely the two
textbook biases.

\paragraph{Arithmetic as a source of exactly solvable channels.}
Uniform covers transport an entire information profile --- every R\'enyi order at once --- from a
small quotient to any covering situation. Since Galois theory supplies uniform covers in abundance,
the profile of a splitting-type channel is a property of the Galois group and the type function,
never of the particular conductor.

\paragraph{Exactness, and two species of loss.}
Every quantity here is exact: no asymptotic constants, no numerically estimated values. The pairing
defect is genuine conditional entropy --- the ambiguous configuration is a fair coin worth one bit,
occurring with probability $4/9$ --- while the which-factor wall is the vanishing of an information
quantity for structural reasons. Rationality, finally, is a low-degree accident:
Theorem~\ref{thm:rigid} shows $4/9$ is the last rational defect.

\section{Future work}

Settled in the present work: rational-defect rigidity at prime degree; full functional (hence
R\'enyi) invariance under uniform covers; and the cyclic form of the which-factor wall for free
transitive actions of any order. Open directions:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Non-abelian channels.} For non-abelian Galois extensions the type is a Frobenius
conjugacy class with law $(|C|/|G|)_C$; the uniform-cover mechanism should survive verbatim, while
the pairing analysis will not, since conjugacy classes do not multiply.
\item \textbf{Higher arity.} For $n=p_1\cdots p_k$ the observable is a $k$-fold convolution on
$C_q$; the defect should admit a closed form in terms of the number of nontrivial summands, and
partial orderings (``is $p_1$ split?'') deserve separate treatment.
\item \textbf{Mixed towers and joint channels.} CRT additivity handles coprime degrees within one
cyclotomic field; joint observation across independent conductors, and its interaction with the
pairing defect, is unexplored.
\item \textbf{Effective equidistribution.} Bounding the deviation of the empirical type entropy for
$p\le X$ from its limit, unconditionally and under GRH, would turn exact identities into usable
finite-$X$ inequalities.
\item \textbf{Defect spectra at composite degree.} For composite $d$ the type is the order of a
group element and pairing becomes a convolution on $C_d$; determining the resulting defect in
closed form, and its rationality pattern, is the natural sequel to Theorem~\ref{thm:rigid}.
\end{enumerate}

